            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "Agree, suppressing the focus steal itself is small: only call enter_pager()\r\nwhen the pager was explicitly requested (g</:messages/entered from expanded cmdline), e.g. a pager_focus flag set in those two places and checked in set_pos().\r\n\r\nThe rest of the new code is answering this question: \r\nwhat dismisses a pager that was never entered? The hide logic (WinEnter autocmd) is only armed inside enter_pager(), \r\nso with just the suppression, the unfocused pager stays on screen until the user enters and leaves it. \r\npager_on_key mirrors cmd_on_key for that: keep it open while in the cmdline (so consecutive command outputs still append, like now), enter on `g<`, dismiss on any other typed key.\r\n\r\nIf it is acceptble that the unfocused pager just stays visible until `q/g<`, \r\nI'm happy to drop pager_on_key and reduce this PR to the minimal version. Or maybe there is a simpler dismissal path I am missing?\r\n",
